			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">&lt;Many years ago&gt;
<br />

<br />

<br />

<br />
&quot;Oh Reyia...&quot;Antilles breathed, moving closer to her and holding her tightly in his arms. His hands moved up and stroked her hair as he gently tilted her forward for another kiss. Reyia sighed in pure bliss. She reached for him and pulled him against her, delighting in the sensation of his rough and calloused skin against hers....
<br />

<br />
Reyia's eyes suddenly flashed open. It was still dark and Antilles stood over her,  regarding her solemnly. &quot;Wake up, Reyia.&quot; he spoke &quot;It is time to travel again.&quot; With a frustrated groan, Reyia struggled to her feet and stared openly at the man who had managed to invade her dreams for the past two nights. It was two days ago when, in East Freeport, the dwarf Derriendin had brought her the unpleasant news that her father wished to see her in person. And worse still was the fact that he waited for her on Faydwer. Antilles was returning home to Kelethin, and had offered to escort her at least as far as the Greater Faydark. She wished suddenly she had been truthful with Antilles about the reason she was traveling to Faydwer, instead of mumbling something about seeing some of Derriendin's guildmates. Antilles had stared deeply into her eyes for a moment and then nodded. Funny, she had never had felt uncomfortable lying before...but this man brought that out in her. 
<br />

<br />
   She trudged along slowly through the Butcherblock Mountains behind him, her ankle now mended enough to allow her somewhat smooth traveling, lost in thought. By tonight, at the latest, they would be in Faydark, and she would be rid of this Fier Dal. The thought should have comforted her but she found herself thinking more and more about the easy way he smiled at the young dwarf warriors training outside Kaladim, the gentle way that he had just yesterday escorted her past the frowning guards at the Kaladim docks, or...she shivered softly...the way he smelt. 
<br />

<br />
    A small wail broke her out of her dangerous thoughts. Antilles had already heard it and was kneeling beside a young Koala Dal elf who clutched his chest and was crying in pain. Antilles leaned in close to the elf and nodded, then helped him to his feet and guided him over by several dwarven guards. Reyia whispered to Antilles &quot;What happened?&quot; Antilles looked grim. &quot;He said he was ambushed while traveling through Dagnor's Cauldron by a pair of aqua goblins warriors. Cowardly those beasts are, and they enjoy attacking those unable to adequately defend themselves.&quot; Antilles nodded and turned in direction &quot;This time, however, they will pay for their crime. Wait here with him, and I will return soon.&quot; Antilles started to run off but she called after him &quot;No, I wish to come with you.&quot; Antilles frowned at her, and Reyia saw the concern in his eyes, &quot;No, I think that you are still too young to face creatures of their skill. I would not wish you any harm before you were safely escorted to the Faydark.&quot;
<br />

<br />
Reyia smiled softly &quot;You don't have a choice. For I am following you there, and if you leave me behind with your speed, I will follow as best I can. And of course, I would be a lot safer by your side than in this wilderness alone. There might be bandits about, you know.&quot; She chuckles quietly at Antilles. He shook his head at her. &quot;As you wish.&quot;
<br />

<br />
    They ended up staying four days in Dagnor's cauldron hunting goblins. Reyia marveled at how Antilles would switch so quickly from his almost lazy, carefree, friendly,  caring manner into an almost vicious,  quick, and expert swordsman. The goblins couldn't touch him. He moved like a wind, dodging and parrying their blows, and slashing them with his weapons. But the time passed and they left the area headed back towards the Faydarks. They reached the zone a day later and came to the part where they would split up. Antilles smiled at Reyia &quot;Good luck to you, lass. It was pleasant traveling with you, and if you ever have need of my services, I will do my best to oblige you.&quot; He bowed low and turned to leave. Reyia thought desperately Don't go...and then managed a barely audible &quot;Antilles.&quot; He turned to look at her...and she stared deeply into his eyes. &quot;Antilles....I love you.&quot;
<br />

<br />
His eyes widened quickly and he stared at her. &quot;I know what you are thinking&quot; spoke Reyia quickly &quot;but we can work this out. I have been told that if I end the lives of enough of the orcs that share the land with you, then the Fier Dal will gradually turn to look on me more favorably. Please. I have never felt so strongly about something in all my life. Please give us a chance.&quot; Antilles looked at her and opened his mouth...and then closed it again. He turned away from her and Reyia felt the hope that had sprung up inside of her fade away until nothing was left but a bitter icy black pit. &quot;Reyia&quot; he spoke at last &quot;I am very fond of you. You have been a wonderful companion, and believe me, the reason I have to say no isn't because I don't think you are a lovely, intelligent, and courageous woman. And it isn't because you are Tier Dal, I have long believed and witnessed men and women doing good and evil of every race. What is inside someone matters much more than what their outside appearance is. Reyia..I am sorry...I just don't love you that way. Not the way you love me.&quot; Antilles looked frustrated and then quickly made his way off. Reyia dropped down to her knees on the soft mossy ground and wept. An ocean of tears flooded from her eyes onto the ground, and she did not move from that spot for several days. 
<br />

<br />
To be continued.
